<section>
	<h2 id="pages">Pages &amp; Patterns</h2>
	<p>
		Every section of a VetPress page is a block pattern: a ready-made group of blocks you insert, then edit like any other content. You can change its text and photos, move it up or down, restyle it or delete it.
	</p>

	<h3 id="building-a-page">Building a Page</h3>
	<ol>
		<li>Go to <strong>Pages &gt; Add New Page</strong>. Click the <strong>+</strong> button at the top left, open the <strong>Patterns</strong> tab, choose <strong>VetPress pages</strong> and click the page you want. If WordPress opens a list of starting patterns for the new page, you can pick the page there instead.</li>
		<li>In the page settings, set <strong>Template</strong> to <strong>Landing (no title)</strong>. The page patterns bring their own title area, so they look right only with this template.</li>
		<li>Replace the demo text and the photo placeholders with your own, then publish.</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		To use a page as your homepage, go to <strong>Settings &gt; Reading</strong>, choose <strong>A static page</strong> and select it as the homepage. Select the page that should list your posts as the posts page.
	</p>
	<p>
		To add a section to any page, click the <strong>+</strong> button at the top left of the editor, open the <strong>Patterns</strong> tab and drag a pattern into the page.
	</p>
	<img class="aligncenter" src="images/patterns.png" alt="VetPress patterns in the block inserter">

	<h3 id="page-patterns">Page Patterns</h3>
	<p>
		The <strong>VetPress pages</strong> category has a whole page for each page of the demo:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Home page</strong>: today's patients, the services fee board, a first visit timeline, the team, wellness plans, reviews, the journal and questions.</li>
		<li><strong>About page</strong>: the clinic's story, the team, patients of the month and testimonials.</li>
		<li><strong>Services page</strong>: the fee board, a first visit by the clock, questions and a booking call to action.</li>
		<li><strong>Service page</strong>: one service in depth, written for dental care, with what the fee covers, the day by the clock and a written estimate.</li>
		<li><strong>Grooming and daycare page</strong>: a salon hero, the grooming menu priced by dog size, a daycare day by the clock, report cards and daycare passes.</li>
		<li><strong>Team page</strong>: every staff badge, plus an open role pinned up as a blank badge.</li>
		<li><strong>Staff profile page</strong>: one team member's badge and bio, their week from the appointment book and their own pets.</li>
		<li><strong>Wellness plans page</strong>: plans, questions and a booking call to action.</li>
		<li><strong>Book a visit page</strong>: a request card for your booking form beside what to bring, opening hours, the emergency line and what happens next.</li>
		<li><strong>Contact page</strong>: contact cards, opening hours and directions.</li>
	</ul>
	<div class="info">
		On a staff profile, each working day in the week strip is a spacer above a highlighted block. One hour is 1.75rem: the spacer's height sets the start time, counted from 07:00, and the highlighted block's minimum height sets the length of the shift.
	</div>

	<h3 id="section-patterns">Section Patterns</h3>
	<p>
		The single sections are in the usual pattern categories:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Banners</strong>: three heroes (today's patients as kennel cards, a leaping dog, and grooming and daycare) and the page header for Landing pages.</li>
		<li><strong>Services</strong> and <strong>Pricing</strong>: the services fee board, the grooming menu, wellness plans and daycare passes.</li>
		<li><strong>About</strong>: a founder's card, clinic facts, a first visit timeline and a day at daycare.</li>
		<li><strong>Team</strong>: four team badges, or the full roster of eight.</li>
		<li><strong>Testimonials</strong>, <strong>Gallery</strong> and <strong>FAQ</strong>: reviews, daycare report cards, patients of the month and questions.</li>
		<li><strong>Contact</strong> and <strong>Call to action</strong>: contact cards, hours and location, the emergency line and the booking call to action.</li>
		<li><strong>Posts</strong>: the latest posts from your journal.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Each photo spot shows a placeholder until you replace it. Select the image and click <strong>Replace</strong> in its toolbar. Remember to describe your photo in the image's <strong>Alternative text</strong> setting.
	</p>

	<h3 id="page-links">Page Links</h3>
	<p>
		The theme's buttons and links point to the demo's page addresses, so create pages at the same addresses to keep them working:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>The <strong>Book a visit</strong> buttons link to <code>/book/</code>. Build that page from the <strong>Book a visit page</strong> pattern and add your booking form to its request card. See <a href="#contact-form-7">Contact Form 7</a>.</li>
		<li>The fee board links each service to a page under <code>/services/</code>, and grooming and daycare to <code>/grooming-and-daycare/</code>.</li>
		<li>Other buttons and the footer links go to <code>/team/</code>, <code>/contact/</code> and the other demo addresses.</li>
		<li>The journal links go to your posts page.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		To point a button somewhere else, select it and edit its link. After a <a href="#demo-content">demo import</a>, these links go to the imported pages, wherever they were saved.
	</p>
</section>
